Chapter: Parsmi--CodexIrHarness

 The compiler as a program that emits IR: `codexir < prog.codex 2> prog.ir`.

 CodexZigHarness beside this one is the same compiler with the zig emitter
 behind it. This one stops at the wire. What that buys is an oracle at THIS
 CHECKOUT'S PIN: the IR bank under `$CODEX_GOLDS/ir` was cut by bare metal at
 an older Update, so a disagreement with it cannot be attributed without a
 second arm compiled from the same source. This is that arm.

 IT IS BUILT WITHOUT A GUEST, which is the whole reason it is cheap. The
 ladder built its codexir through the seed -- bundle, compile to IR on bare
 metal, push that through the ring plug, build the emitted zig -- because the
 seed emits x86 and there was no other way to a first zig. `codexzig` exists
 now and is a verified fixed point, so the chain is three host steps: swap the
 harness, transpile, `zig build-exe`. Two minutes, no QEMU.

Section: Halt

 The same gate CodexZigHarness carries, for the same reason and in the same
 words. The driver does not emit when the bag has errors, and a tool that
 emitted anyway once had us report a compiler soundness hole to Damian's lane
 that was this harness being deaf: `native/codexzig`, the same compiler from
 the same plug whose harness DID read the bag, refused the same program with
 the same code. CODEGEN-HALTED is the marker a reader refuses on by name.

  cir-halted : List Diagnostic -> Text
  cir-halted (es) =
   let n = list-length es
   in let e0 = list-at es 0
   in "CODEGEN-HALTED: " & show n & " error(s); no IR emitted; first CDX" & show (e0.code) & " " & (e0.message) & "\n"

 THE NON-ERROR DIAGNOSTICS GO TO STDOUT and the IR goes to stderr, because
 `print-text` is `std.debug.print`, which is stderr -- and stderr is where this
 tool's output goes. A warning printed the ordinary way would land inside the
 IR. `write-binary` is the only route to fd 1 from Codex and it takes raw
 bytes. Infos are counted, not printed: fifteen units once carried 3,685
 CDX4010 `bounds proven` infos against ten warnings worth reading.

  cir-sev-word : Integer -> Text
  cir-sev-word (s) = if s == sev-warning then "warning" else "notice"

  cir-notice-line : Diagnostic -> Text
  cir-notice-line (d) = "CDX" & show (d.code) & " " & cir-sev-word (d.severity) & ": " & (d.message)

  cir-line-bytes : Text -> List Integer
  cir-line-bytes (t) = list-push (text-to-utf8-bytes t) 10

  cir-notices : List Diagnostic, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  cir-notices (ds) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length ds then acc
   else let d = list-at ds i
   in if (d.severity) == sev-info then cir-notices ds (i + 1) acc
   else cir-notices ds (i + 1) (acc & cir-line-bytes (cir-notice-line d))

  cir-info-count : List Diagnostic, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cir-info-count (ds) (i) (n) =
   if i >= list-length ds then n
   else let d = list-at ds i
   in if (d.severity) == sev-info then cir-info-count ds (i + 1) (n + 1)
   else cir-info-count ds (i + 1) n

  cir-report : DiagnosticBag -> List Integer
  cir-report (b) =
   let ds = bag-diagnostics b
   in let body = cir-notices ds 0 []
   in let n = cir-info-count ds 0 0
   in if n == 0 then body
   else body & cir-line-bytes ("codexir: " & show n & " info diagnostics not shown")

Section: Roots

 opening.codex's own list, copied because that chapter cannot ride beside a
 harness that defines `opening`. A copy that drifts prunes a root and loses it
 from the IR silently: this list carried four names instead of six for weeks,
 and CodexZigHarness had the SAME truncation, so the two arms agreed with each
 other and neither could see it. An oracle cannot find a mistake both of its
 arms make.

  cir-emit-roots : List Text
  cir-emit-roots = ["opening", "vb-capacity-auto", "vb-read-auto", "vb-write-auto", "fat16-servicer-read", "fat16-servicer-write"]

Section: Driver

 THIS IS `emit-ir-uni`, the driver's own IR mode, and not a re-derivation of
 its phases: `compile-frontend-ir`, then `lift-ir-for-emit`, then the prune.

 `compile-frontend-ir` AND NOT `compile-frontend-cdx`, which is the one choice
 here that decides what the IR CONTAINS. The `-cdx` sequence runs a RESOLVE
 phase the `-ir` sequence does not, and running it rewrites every let binding
 whose nullary ConstructedTy names a record into a RecordTy the driver leaves
 as `ctd` -- 930 lines of difference on the ir_to_x86 subject, and an IR wire
 the oracle path never emits. CodexZigHarness calls `-cdx` deliberately,
 because it is feeding an emitter and not a bank.

 THE CHAPTER NAME IS "Program", which is the driver's own literal and is
 measured rather than assumed: bare metal's IR for this repository's own
 subject opens `(chapter "Program")`. The ladder's bank names the entry
 chapter instead, and that is the LADDER'S harness, not the compiler.

  opening : [Console, FileSystem] Nothing = act
    src <- read-file-uni "/dev/stdin"
    let mountain-base = init-phase-allocator
    in let deck-base = __heap-save
    in let deck-set = __deck-set deck-base
    in let deck-adv = __heap-advance 536870912
    in let fe = compile-frontend-ir src "Program" compile-flags-default
    in if bag-has-errors (fe.bag) then print-text (cir-halted (bag-errors (fe.bag)))
    else let lifted-ir = lift-ir-for-emit (fe.ir) compile-flags-default
    in act
      write-binary (cir-report (fe.bag))
      print-text (emit-ir-chapter (ir-prune-unreachable-roots lifted-ir cir-emit-roots) (fe.text-meta) (fe.type-defs))
    end
  end
